---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import ComponentPage from "../../../layouts/ComponentPage.astro";
import FieldDemo from "../../../components/FieldDemo";
import { codeTheme } from "../../../components/code-theme";

const renderPropExample = `import { Field } from "@/components/ui/field";

<Field
  label="Email"
  description="We only use it to send the newsletter."
  error={errors.email}
  required
>
  {(control) => <input type="email" {...control} />}
</Field>`;

const childExample = `<Field label="Message" description="Up to 500 characters.">
  <textarea id="message" aria-describedby="message-policy" />
</Field>`;
---

<ComponentPage slug="field">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    It renders a real <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> and links the help and error text by id, so you
    never wire <code>aria-describedby</code> by hand. It does not import any control, so it works
    with any native input, textarea or select.
  </Fragment>

  <FieldDemo slot="demo" client:visible />

  <Fragment slot="usage">
    <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Usage</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      The main API is a render prop: a function that receives the control props and spreads them
      onto your element.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={renderPropExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      A single element child also works. Field adds the same props with <code>cloneElement</code>:
      it keeps the child's own <code>id</code> and adds its ids to the child's own
      <code>aria-describedby</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={childExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">What it wires</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        <strong>An id</strong> for the control, generated unless the child has one, and the label's
        <code>for</code>. Clicking the label focuses the control.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A description:</strong> <code>aria-describedby</code> lists the description, then
        the error. Screen readers read them after the name.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The invalid state:</strong> <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> while{" "}
        <code>error</code> is set. Style the invalid border from it, but the error text is what
        tells users what to fix.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The required state:</strong> the native <code>required</code> attribute when the
        field is required.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Errors</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      The error shows below the control with an icon and text in the destructive color, so color is
      never the only signal. Screen readers hear it with an "Error:" prefix.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      It sits in a region with <code>aria-live="polite"</code> that is always in the page, even
      while empty. Screen readers only announce changes to a live region that already exists, so an
      error inserted together with its region is often missed. When a form fails to submit, also
      move focus to the first invalid field, as the demo does.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Required</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      A required field shows "(required)" in text next to the label, never a color or a lone
      asterisk. The control gets the native <code>required</code> attribute, which screen readers
      announce, so the marker is hidden from them to avoid hearing it twice. Native validation
      bubbles still run on submit unless the form has <code>noValidate</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      With a render prop, spread every prop you receive, <code>required</code> included. If the
      rendered control is neither <code>required</code> nor <code>aria-required="true"</code>, the
      field lets screen readers read the marker as part of the label, so the requirement is never
      lost, and development builds log a warning that names the control.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <p slot="install-notes" class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    It creates <code>components/ui/field.tsx</code> and needs only your <code>cn()</code> helper. If
    your
    <code>lib/utils.ts</code> differs from the current shadcn version, the CLI asks before replacing
    it; answering no is safe.
  </p>
</ComponentPage>
